CHAPTER V

WOMEN PROTECTION

Part One General

Article 11

1. The Regional Government is responsible for providing Women Protection including the following efforts:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for Women Protection;
b. making efforts to prevent the occurrence of Violence against Women;
c. making efforts to prevent the occurrence of criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
d. improving the effectiveness of services for Women and Children victims of violence;
e. enhancing protection efforts for Women workers;
f. strengthening and developing institutions providing protection services for Women workers;
g. strengthening and developing institutions providing Women Protection services;
h. conducting cooperation with the government, Regional Government, other Regional Governments, private parties, business sector, and/or community non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women; and
i. involving the active role of families, religious leaders, the Community and institutions in the field of Women Protection.
2. The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in accordance with their duties and functions.

1. GENERAL

In order to realize Women who are capable physically, mentally, spiritually, socially, in knowledge, and in skills so that they are ready to be empowered, as well as efforts to protect Women and Children from all acts of Violence in order to achieve the goal of welfare through a Regional regulation that can serve as a reference for all parties related to Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

The formulation of Regional regulations concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection has the intent and purpose as the implementation of the broadest Regional autonomy, so the Women's Empowerment and Child Protection sector is one of the sectors that is quite important to be managed and regulated in order to realize Women who are capable physically, mentally, spiritually, socially, in knowledge, and in skills so that they are ready to be empowered, as well as efforts to protect Women and Children from all acts of Violence in order to achieve the goal of welfare through a draft Regional regulation that can serve as a reference for all parties related to Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

The establishment of this Regional regulation is based on the principles of good formation of legislation and the principles of material content of legislation.

The implementation of governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to increase efforts to Protect the rights of Women, fulfill Children's Rights and Special Protection of Children and increase the role of Regional Government and the business world, media and the Community in efforts to Empower Women and Protect Children. The principal matters contained in the body of the Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Empowerment and Protection of Women and Children include:
a. Women's Empowerment;
b. Women's Protection;
c. Child Protection;
d. safe houses;
e. Women's Empowerment and Child Protection by Village government;
f. family quality;
g. cooperation; h. awards;
i. Community participation;
j. financing;
k. prohibitions;
1. investigation provisions; danm. criminal provisions.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government, NGOs/Social Organizations, the Community and parents/families shall have the obligation to provide Child Protection consisting of:
a. Children in the womb;
b. Toddlers;
c. School-Age Children;
d. Neglected and street children; and
e. Children requiring Special Protection.
2. The implementation of Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. policies, programs, and mechanisms concerning:
1. prevention, supervision, complaints/reporting, and development of Child data information systems;
2. integrated handling for Children who are victims of violence, maltreatment, exploitation and neglect;
3. guarantee of fulfillment of the rights of every Child who becomes a victim of crime, violence, maltreatment, exploitation and neglect including:
a. recovery services and health maintenance;
b) continuity of education services;
c. psychosocial services; and
d. legal aid services.
4. implementation of family support including:
a. counseling;
b. child-rearing education;
c. family mediation; and
d. economic support.
b. raising awareness and attitudes of the Community through socialization, education, and information regarding:
1. Children's Rights, Child Protection, prevention of child marriage and Child rearing; and
2. the impact of violence, exploitation, maltreatment, and Child neglect.
c. enhancing the capacity of Child Protection services which includes the development of institutional capacity and service provider personnel.
d. service provider personnel as referred to in letter c includes:
1. health;
2. education;
3. psychosocial;
4. child-rearing; and
5. legal aid.
e. enhancing the ability of Children to recognize risks and dangers from situations or acts that may cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment, and neglect.
3. The implementation of Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (2) letters a through e shall be carried out and/or coordinated by the SKPD responsible for Child Protection affairs.

Part Three Handling

Article 23

1. The targets of Child handling include:
a. Children outside the care of parents;
b. Children in disaster emergency situations;
c. Children in conflict with the law;
d. Child victims of physical, mental and/or sexual Violence;
e. Children subjected to maltreatment and neglect;
f. Child victims of economic exploitation;
g. Children who become victims of the criminal act of trafficking in persons;
h. Children who become victims of gambling, abuse of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (NAPZA); and
i. Children who are in situations where the nature and condition of the workplace may endanger the health, safety, and morals of the Child.
2. Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:
a. identifying and receiving complaints/reports;
b. rescue actions;
c. placement of Children in temporary shelters;
d. rehabilitation including:
1. health recovery services;
2. psychosocial recovery services; and
3. legal assistance/expert witness assistance.
e. social reintegration in the form of post-rehabilitation service support.

CHAPTER X COOPERATION

Article 49

1. In efforts to implement Women's Empowerment and Child Protection, the Regional Government may cooperate.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted with:
a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other Regional Governments;
d. vertical agencies;
e. advocates;
f. social workers;
g. volunteer companions;
h. spiritual counselors; and
i. non-governmental organizations engaged in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
3. Forms of cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. socialization;
b. provision of legal assistance;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation; and
g. spiritual guidance.
4. Procedures for implementing cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER XIV PROHIBITIONS

Article 54

a. treating Women and Children in a discriminatory manner that results in Women and Children suffering losses, both material and moral, thereby hindering their social function;
b. treating persons with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of Children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing Violence against Women and Children;
e. committing Violence or threat of Violence to force Women and Children to have sexual intercourse with him or her or with another person;
f. committing Violence or threat of violence, forcing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading Women and Children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of Women and Children;
h. obstructing Women and Children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing their religious teachings and/or using their own language without neglecting access to Community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of Women and Children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving Women and Children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances; and/or
k. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of Women and Children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

Article 38

1. Special Protection for Children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 36 letter b includes Children in conflict with the law and Child victims of criminal acts, which is the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government, Police, Prosecutor's Office, Court, Parents, Family and Community.
2. Handling of Children in conflict with the legal process and implementation of Judge's decisions must continue to prioritize the Rights of the Child.
